Most DECS CD boot NT / RC3 2000, Alpha version of course,  only, at 
least the ones I have seen.  You will need a milo boot disk for your 
machine type.  I have a 164LX and use ARC, you'll probably be using SRM 
and aboot.  I have an optical drive that I loaded my basedebs, boot 
floppys, drivers and some other tarball (that's a funny word, TAR BALLS) 
onto.  I booted from the milo boot disk, booted linux.bin off the 
optical and it ran almost flawlessly from there, it wanted the tar files 
in certain dorectories so with some creative mouting and moving of the 
files I got it to pick up on them.  Once installed I used dselect and 
got the debian updates, then I dled / compiled 2.4.23, then I made the 
switch to Debian testing tree by modifying /ect/apt/preferences and 
apt.sources.

Loading linux on an Alpha can be extremely time consuming and I've seen 
alot of people give up.  But out of all the X86 linux guys I persoanlly 
know, I'ld say 1 of them could load up an Alpha, the rest would commit 
suicide.  Linux on anything after that is a breeze, except of course 
maybe the AS/400 / iSeries.  :D

